{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The book has 300 pages, derived by partitioning the 792 digits into three blocks: 9 one-digit page numbers (pages 1–9) using 9 digits, 90 two-digit page numbers (pages 10–99) using 180 digits, and the remaining 603 digits assigned to three-digit page numbers, which exactly accommodates 201 pages (pages 100–300).","counterconditions":["If front matter (e.g., roman numerals) consumed part of the 792-digit budget, then fewer digits remain for arabic-numbered pages and the page count falls below 300.","If 'pages' means physical leaves rather than numbered sides, the count of leaves would be 150, not 300.","If facing-page spreads are counted once per spread instead of once per page, the allocation of digits to physical sheets changes and the arithmetic no longer holds.","If the page numbering skips any numbers (e.g., blank unnumbered pages or plates counted separately), the linear digit-length sum is disrupted."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Total digits D(n) = sum_{k=1}^{n} len(k). Split into blocks by digit length: 9×1 = 9 digits for 1–9, 90×2 = 180 digits for 10–99. Remainder after the two-digit block: 792 − 9 − 180 = 603. Since each subsequent page number uses 3 digits, the count of three-digit pages is 603 / 3 = 201. Adding to the base of 99 gives 99 + 201 = 300 pages. The remainder after division by 3 is exactly zero, so the digit budget is exactly exhausted with no partial page.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.98}],"simulation_proposals":[]}